+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/docs/ConsoleReference.md
@@ -185,6 +185,39 @@ The measurement is written up in
 dome-only sky, illuminance read off `r_HDRDebug 1`, and the factor that brings it to a real
 clear-sky diffuse value (about 12 000 lux with the sun at 45 degrees).
 
+### r_SceneReferredWriterClamp
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 32768 (2^15) |
+| Purpose | The ceiling every scene-referred **writer** is held to, in exposed engine units, and the ceiling the 709 -> AP1 conversion enforces on the whole HDR target. Active only while the scene-referred path is on (stage 2 and up). 0 disables both the clamps and the guard. |
+
+A **format** bound, not a look. The HDR target is fp16 and stops at 65504. Two writers can exceed
+that once the pre-exposure multiplies them up:
+
+- the procedural sky's **Mie forward-scattering peak**. There is no sun sprite in the sky pass -
+  what reads as the sun *is* `pow(miePart_g2_1 + miePart_g_2 * cosine, -1.5)`, which diverges as
+  the view direction meets the sun. Stock CRYENGINE never noticed, because the tone map's exposure
+  divided it down before anything looked at it.
+- **emissive materials**, authored in kcd/m2, at a night pre-exposure of a couple of thousand.
+
+An `Inf` in the buffer is not a bright pixel: nothing downstream reads single texels, everything
+averages. One `Inf` takes a whole bloom downsample block, a whole glare threshold neighbourhood,
+or - through the meter's log mean - the whole exposure loop. Each writer is clamped where it is,
+and `ExecuteSceneReferredConversion` enforces the same ceiling on the entire target as a second
+line (NaN reads as black, `Inf` and anything over reads as the ceiling), because the writer list
+can never be proved complete.
+
+2^15 rather than 2^16: 65536 is not representable in fp16, so a writer clamped "at 2^16" would
+still store `Inf`. 2^15 leaves one doubling of headroom for the blends that follow. In stops it is
+17.5 over mid grey, against the camera's own **Sensor Clip** at 14 - so the buffer can hold three
+and a half stops more than the picture can ever show. Setting this to 0 is the way to *see* the
+overflow it exists to prevent (a flat white "bun" where the sun should be, and sun shafts over the
+whole screen).
+
+The guard sits in the `else` of `r_SceneReferredDebug 2`, so that mode still shows texels that
+arrive broken rather than texels this pass already repaired.
+
